questions.stem_md:
Draw the graph of $y = \cos^{-1} x$ using at least five points. From your graph:

(a) state the domain and range;

(b) say whether the function is increasing or decreasing;

(c) find the value of $x$ at which $\cos^{-1} x = \dfrac{\pi}{2}$;

(d) explain how the graph is related to that of $y = \cos x$, $0 \le x \le \pi$.

Find the values of:

(i) $\sin^{-1}\left(\sin\dfrac{4\pi}{5}\right)$;

(ii) $\cos^{-1}\left(\cos\left(-\dfrac{3\pi}{4}\right)\right)$;

(iii) $\tan^{-1}\left(\tan\dfrac{4\pi}{3}\right)$.

(iv) In which of (i)–(iii) is the answer equal to the angle written inside? Explain using the principal value branches.

Without a calculator, find the exact value of each expression, explaining the sign in each case:

(i) $\cos\left(\tan^{-1}\dfrac{12}{5}\right)$;

(ii) $\sin\left(\cos^{-1}\left(-\dfrac{4}{5}\right)\right)$;

(iii) $\tan\left(\cos^{-1}\left(-\dfrac{4}{5}\right)\right)$;

(iv) $\sec\left(\sin^{-1}\left(-\dfrac{7}{25}\right)\right)$.

Find the domain of each function:

(i) $f(x) = \sin^{-1}(2x + 3)$;

(ii) $g(x) = \cos^{-1}(1 - 2x)$;

(iii) $h(x) = \sin^{-1}(x^2 - 3)$.

(iv) For which real $x$ are both $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ defined?

Solve each equation, or show that it has no solution:

(i) $\sin^{-1}(3x - 1) = -\dfrac{\pi}{6}$;

(ii) $\cos^{-1}\left(\dfrac{x}{2}\right) = \dfrac{5\pi}{6}$;

(iii) $\tan^{-1}(x^2 - 2) = \dfrac{\pi}{4}$;

(iv) $\cos^{-1} x = -\dfrac{\pi}{3}$.
